WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.920
Welcome to the Cell and Biomolecule data workshop.

00:00:03.500 --> 00:00:06.900
A membrane protein can help transport a substance without making the process

00:00:06.900 --> 00:00:07.320
active.

00:00:07.960 --> 00:00:12.080
In facilitated diffusion, a suitable channel or carrier supports movement down

00:00:12.080 --> 00:00:15.900
the relevant gradient without directly coupling transport to an energy source.

00:00:16.880 --> 00:00:20.820
Active transport moves substances against a gradient by coupling that movement

00:00:20.820 --> 00:00:22.820
to energy, directly or indirectly.

00:00:23.400 --> 00:00:27.560
For an ion, concentration alone is not the whole story: electrical potential

00:00:27.560 --> 00:00:29.960
also contributes to the electrochemical gradient.

00:00:30.800 --> 00:00:34.935
Therefore, a question stating only that a membrane protein is involved does

00:00:34.935 --> 00:00:35.100
not

00:00:35.100 --> 00:00:36.940
uniquely identify active transport.

00:00:38.260 --> 00:00:41.680
Read the direction, transported substance and energy coupling before

00:00:41.680 --> 00:00:42.720
classifying the mechanism.

00:00:43.056 --> 00:00:47.496
Suppose a cell membrane permits water but not the solute being compared.

00:00:48.356 --> 00:00:52.476
If the external solution has a higher effective concentration of that solute,

00:00:53.116 --> 00:00:54.656
water tends to leave the cell.

00:00:55.216 --> 00:00:56.656
An animal cell may shrink.

00:00:57.476 --> 00:01:02.256
In a hypotonic external solution, net water entry can swell an animal cell,

00:01:02.896 --> 00:01:06.996
whereas a plant cell's wall resists expansion and supports turgor.

00:01:07.856 --> 00:01:12.324
An isotonic condition means no sustained net volume change in the simple

00:01:12.324 --> 00:01:12.696
model,

00:01:13.056 --> 00:01:15.936
not that individual water molecules stop moving.

00:01:16.816 --> 00:01:21.376
If the solute can readily cross, its concentration alone may not predict the

00:01:21.376 --> 00:01:22.736
long term volume response.

00:01:23.756 --> 00:01:26.836
State permeability and cell type before choosing a diagram.

00:01:27.168 --> 00:01:31.703
An organelle question often gives a functional clue rather than a complete

00:01:31.703 --> 00:01:31.948
cell

00:01:31.948 --> 00:01:32.368
identity.

00:01:33.508 --> 00:01:35.048
Ribosomes carry out translation.

00:01:35.988 --> 00:01:40.168
Ribosomes associated with rough endoplasmic reticulum help make many proteins

00:01:40.168 --> 00:01:44.908
entering the secretory pathway, and the Golgi apparatus modifies and sorts

00:01:44.908 --> 00:01:46.088
material in that pathway.

00:01:47.108 --> 00:01:51.528
Mitochondria support major stages of aerobic energy metabolism, but finding

00:01:51.528 --> 00:01:55.528
many mitochondria does not prove that a cell has no other metabolic pathways.

00:01:55.528 --> 00:01:59.894
A cell lacking a visible nucleus in one image is not automatically

00:01:59.894 --> 00:02:00.688
prokaryotic:

00:02:01.428 --> 00:02:05.208
the section, imaging method or cell type may explain the observation.

00:02:06.208 --> 00:02:10.208
Combine structural evidence with the stated function instead of treating one

00:02:10.208 --> 00:02:11.988
image feature as universal proof.

00:02:12.216 --> 00:02:16.236
An energy diagram shows the same reactants and products with two different

00:02:16.236 --> 00:02:16.476
peaks.

00:02:17.316 --> 00:02:21.296
The lower peak can represent an enzyme catalysed pathway because activation

00:02:21.296 --> 00:02:22.396
energy is reduced.

00:02:23.276 --> 00:02:27.056
The overall free energy difference between the reactants and products remains

00:02:27.056 --> 00:02:27.556
the same.

00:02:28.516 --> 00:02:33.096
Enzymes accelerate approach to equilibrium; they do not, by themselves, change

00:02:33.096 --> 00:02:37.308
the equilibrium position or turn every energetically unfavourable reaction

00:02:37.308 --> 00:02:37.536
into

00:02:37.536 --> 00:02:38.336
a favourable one.

00:02:39.096 --> 00:02:43.403
Energy coupling can support a cellular process, but coupling is a separate

00:02:43.403 --> 00:02:43.636
part

00:02:43.636 --> 00:02:44.556
of the explanation.

00:02:45.536 --> 00:02:49.416
When reading the graph, compare the peak height with the starting level for

00:02:49.416 --> 00:02:53.856
activation energy, and compare starting and ending levels for reaction free

00:02:53.856 --> 00:02:54.196
energy.

00:02:54.384 --> 00:02:58.544
A teaching assay reports product concentration rising from two to eight

00:02:58.544 --> 00:03:01.144
micromoles per litre over the first three minutes.

00:03:01.744 --> 00:03:06.004
The average rate in that interval is six divided by three, or two micromoles

00:03:06.004 --> 00:03:07.084
per litre per minute.

00:03:07.564 --> 00:03:11.625
If the early region is approximately linear, this can estimate the initial

00:03:11.625 --> 00:03:11.844
rate

00:03:11.844 --> 00:03:13.304
over that measurement interval.

00:03:14.044 --> 00:03:17.884
Do not divide the final concentration by time without subtracting the starting

00:03:17.884 --> 00:03:18.224
value.

00:03:18.624 --> 00:03:23.304
A later plateau can reflect substrate depletion, product effects or enzyme

00:03:23.304 --> 00:03:27.344
instability, so an endpoint alone does not give the same information.

00:03:28.484 --> 00:03:32.684
Compare assays using matching units, observation periods and the same defined

00:03:32.684 --> 00:03:33.084
readout.

00:03:33.288 --> 00:03:37.428
At a fixed enzyme amount and suitable conditions, raising substrate

00:03:37.428 --> 00:03:41.908
concentration can increase the initial rate until the enzyme system approaches

00:03:41.908 --> 00:03:42.508
saturation.

00:03:43.288 --> 00:03:47.708
A rate plateau does not mean that catalysis has stopped; it can mean that

00:03:47.708 --> 00:03:50.008
adding more substrate produces little further increase.

00:03:50.628 --> 00:03:54.788
In the simple reversible competitive inhibition model, inhibitor and substrate

00:03:54.788 --> 00:03:58.908
compete for access at the active site, and sufficiently high substrate can

00:03:58.908 --> 00:04:00.648
reduce the inhibitor's relative effect.

00:04:01.088 --> 00:04:03.588
Do not apply that conclusion to every inhibitor.

00:04:04.308 --> 00:04:08.188
A single lowered rate does not uniquely identify an inhibition mechanism.

00:04:09.268 --> 00:04:13.628
Compare the response across conditions and keep enzyme amount, temperature and

00:04:13.628 --> 00:04:14.448
pH controlled before claiming a specific model.

00:04:14.448 --> 00:04:18.688
Two samples show different enzyme activity at different pH values.

00:04:19.328 --> 00:04:23.291
The difference can reflect changes in ionisation of important groups,

00:04:23.291 --> 00:04:23.808
substrate

00:04:23.808 --> 00:04:25.628
binding or structural stability.

00:04:26.368 --> 00:04:30.128
It does not establish a universal optimum pH for all enzymes.

00:04:30.868 --> 00:04:34.768
Likewise, warming can initially increase rate, while sufficiently high

00:04:34.768 --> 00:04:37.948
temperature can disrupt enzyme structure and reduce activity.

00:04:38.268 --> 00:04:42.428
A low rate at one temperature does not alone prove permanent denaturation;

00:04:42.988 --> 00:04:46.888
additional evidence is needed to distinguish reversible effects from

00:04:46.888 --> 00:04:47.868
irreversible damage.

00:04:48.548 --> 00:04:52.928
When testing temperature, keep other conditions comparable, including pH and

00:04:52.928 --> 00:04:53.608
enzyme amount.

00:04:54.688 --> 00:04:57.837
Describe the measured difference first, then state only the mechanism

00:04:57.837 --> 00:04:58.248
supported

00:04:58.248 --> 00:04:59.788
by the experimental design.

00:05:00.768 --> 00:05:06.148
A tissue snapshot contains forty cells visibly in mitosis among five hundred

00:05:06.148 --> 00:05:06.848
counted cells.

00:05:07.728 --> 00:05:10.028
Its mitotic index is eight percent.

00:05:10.028 --> 00:05:14.048
That is an observed fraction, not automatically eight percent of every

00:05:14.048 --> 00:05:15.548
individual cell's cycle time.

00:05:16.728 --> 00:05:20.348
Estimating stage duration from a snapshot requires assumptions about an

00:05:20.348 --> 00:05:25.048
appropriately sampled, asynchronous population, steady behaviour and the

00:05:25.048 --> 00:05:27.208
absence of major selection or arrest effects.

00:05:28.028 --> 00:05:32.928
Interphase is not an inactive waiting period: it includes growth and, during S

00:05:32.928 --> 00:05:34.368
phase, DNA replication.

00:05:35.268 --> 00:05:39.728
If a treatment raises the mitotic index, cells may be entering mitosis faster

00:05:39.728 --> 00:05:42.688
or spending longer there, among other possibilities.

00:05:43.168 --> 00:05:46.128
The fraction alone does not distinguish those mechanisms.
